  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 120 AM CDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nAs southerly winds continue over northeastern SD into early this \nmorning, there will be some stronger wind gusts from the downsloping \nwinds along the eastern edge of the Prairie Coteau. These winds \ncould gust up to 30-40 mph. A weak cold front will be moving into \ncentral SD this morning and then into northeastern SD by the \nafternoon. As this cold front moves in, the winds will turn to \nbecome move northerly, cutting off the downsloping effects. This \nwill cause the wind gusts to decrease to below 20 mph by mid \nmorning. There is drier air located over central and northeastern SD \nand west central MN, which will help to keep rain showers from \nforming as the weak cold front moves through today. \n\nHigh temperatures today will be 5-10 degrees warmer than normal, in \nthe mid to upper 60s. Once the cold front moves through, \ntemperatures will drop slightly, with high temperatures around \nnormal on Monday, in the upper 50s to low 60s. Low temperatures \ntonight into Monday morning will cool slightly from last night, and \nwill be in the mid 30s to low 40s. The coldest temperatures will be \nover north central SD, and could result in patchy areas of frost \nform to form. Winds will turn to be from the southeast Tuesday, \nwhich will help warmer air move into central and northeastern SD \nagain. This will warm temperatures to be slightly above normal again \nfor a couple of days. Additionally, while the dry air will move out \nof the area after Monday, there will still be not enough moisture \nfor rain to develop as other boundaries move through.",
